  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002">
        <exercise id="_6a5dfa57-fef9-41e5-b8cd-c8e299fa4c13" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00002">1. Here is an inequality: \(\frac{7x+6}2\leq3x+2\)Select two of the values that are a solution to the inequality.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">Correct! \(x = -3\), \(x = -2\)</para>
            <para id="para-00004">Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.1. </para>
            <para id="para-00005">The correct answers are \(x = -3\), \(x = -2\).
      </para>
            <para id="para-00006">Substitute the number 1 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 1 hour.
        \(9(1)+11 \gt 15(1)\)
        \(9(1)+11 \gt 15(1)\)
        \(20\gt 15\)
        \(20\gt 15\)
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B. Company A costs more than Company B.
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B. Company A costs more than Company B.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="c160b1e0-fda6-40eb-a8da-4e31848f0df5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">2. Which number line is represented by the inequality \( x ≤ 6 \)?</para>
            <para id="para-00008"> <figure id="fig-00001"><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 6. The number line to the right of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_01.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para id="para-00009"> <figure id="fig-00002"><media alt="A number line is shown. There is a closed circle on 6. The number line to the left of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_02.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para id="para-00010">  <figure id="fig-00003"><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 6. The number line to the left of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_03.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para id="para-00011">  <figure id="fig-00004"><media alt="A number line is shown. There is a closed circle on 6. The number line to the right of 6 is highlighted.
"><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_04.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para id="para-00012">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00013">A.</para>
            <para id="para-00014">B.</para>
            <para id="para-00015">C.</para>
            <para id="para-00016">D.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00017">Correct! <figure id="fig-00005"><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 6. The number line to the left of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_05.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para id="para-00018">Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.2 </para>
            <para id="para-00019">The correct answer is <figure id="fig-00006"><media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 6. The number line to the left of 6 is highlighted."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_06.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para id="para-00020">\(x \le 6\) means that the solution would be any number less than or equal to 6.
        The graph would have a filled in circle on the number 6, because it can be equal to 6. The arrow would point to infinity on the left of the 6, because it is less than 6.
        The graph would have a filled in circle on the number 6, because it can be equal to 6. The arrow would point to infinity on the left of the 6, because it is less than 6.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <para id="para-00021">For questions 3 – 4, use the following information:</para>
      <para id="para-00022">
        <quote id="quote-00001">Scooter rental company A charges $9 per hour plus a $11 initiation fee. Scooter rental company B charges $15 per hour.</quote>
      </para>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00003">
        <exercise id="eadc8e80-78e7-4b3a-bf8a-ae7a81f3a6b4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00023">3. A customer writes the following inequality to represent the situation: \(9h + 11 > 15h\).</para>
            <para id="para-00024">Which of the following is true about the given inequality?</para>
            <para id="para-00025">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00026">Company A costs more than Company B.</para>
            <para id="para-00027">Company A costs less than Company B.</para>
            <para id="para-00028">Company A costs the same as Company B.</para>
            <para id="para-00029">h represents the number of scooters rented.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00030">Correct! Company A costs more than Company B.</para>
            <para id="para-00031">Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.3.
      </para>
            <para id="para-00032">The correct answer is Company A costs more than Company B. </para>
            <para id="para-00033">Substitute the number 1 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 1 hour.
        \(9(1)+11 \gt 15(1)\)
        \(9(1)+11 \gt 15(1)\)
        \(20 \lt 5\)
        \(20 \lt 5\)
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B. Company A costs more than Company B.
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B. Company A costs more than Company B.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_7c8cc139-3f1c-48ef-aa50-44fc5400af42" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00034">4. For which number of hours does Company B cost less?</para>
            <para id="para-00035">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00036">1 hour</para>
            <para id="para-00037">2 hours</para>
            <para id="para-00038">3 hours</para>
            <para id="para-00039">4 hours</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00040">Correct! 1 hour</para>
            <para id="para-00041">Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.3

      </para>
            <para id="para-00042">The correct answer is 1 hour.
      </para>
            <para id="para-00043">
        Substitute the number 1 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 1 hour.
        Company A: 9(1)+11=$20 
        Company A: 9(1)+11=$20 
        Company B: 15(1)=$15 
        Company B: 15(1)=$15 
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B.
      
        To rent a scooter for 1 hour, it costs $20 from Company A and $15 from Company B.
      </para>
            <para id="para-00044">
        Substitute the number 2 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 2 hours.
        Company A: 
        Company A: 
        9(2)+11=$29
        9(2)+11=$29
        Company B: 15(2)=$30
        Company B: 15(2)=$30
        To rent a scooter for 2 hours, it costs $29 from Company A and $30 from Company B.
      
        To rent a scooter for 2 hours, it costs $29 from Company A and $30 from Company B.
      </para>
            <para id="para-00045">
        Substitute the number 3 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 3 hours.
        Company A: 9(3)+11=$38
        Company A: 9(3)+11=$38
        Company B: 15(3)=$45
        Company B: 15(3)=$45
        To rent a scooter for 3 hours, it costs $38 from Company A and $45 from Company B.
      
        To rent a scooter for 3 hours, it costs $38 from Company A and $45 from Company B.
      </para>
            <para id="para-00046">
        Substitute the number 4 in for \(h\) to see how much it would cost to rent a scooter for 4 hours.
        Company A: 9(4)+11=$47
        Company A: 9(4)+11=$47
        Company B: 15(4)=$60
        Company B: 15(4)=$60
        To rent a scooter for 4 hours, it costs $47 from Company A and $60 from Company B.
      
        To rent a scooter for 4 hours, it costs $47 from Company A and $60 from Company B.
      </para>
            <para id="para-00047">Company B costs the least for 1 hour.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_41abc371-b9ff-4743-8f77-ac06b17d1353" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00005">
            <para id="para-00048">For questions 5 – 7, use the following information:</para>
            <para id="para-00049">
              <quote id="quote-00002">Cycle Rentals offers 20 membership points for each day that a customer rents a bike. Bikes Unlimited offers 5 membership points for each day that a customer rents a bike plus 60 points for joining the membership club.</quote>
            </para>
            <para id="para-00050">5. The equation \(20x = 5x + 60\) represents a possible constraint about the situation. Solve for \(x\).</para>
            <para id="para-00051">\(x\) = ____ </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00005">
            <para id="para-00052">Correct! The correct answer is 4. If you rent a bike for longer than 4 days, you earn more membership points at Cycle Rentals.</para>
            <para id="para-00053">
        \(20x=5x+60\)
        \(15x=60\)
        \(15x=60\)
        \(x=4 \) 
        \(x=4 \) 
      
      </para>
            <para id="para-00054">Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.4. </para>
            <para id="para-00055">The correct answer is 4. If you rent a bike for longer than 4 days, you earn more membership points at Cycle Rentals.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00004">
        <exercise id="bc34b533-547f-47a7-942e-01f28d487514" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00006">
            <para id="para-00056">6. Which shop offers more membership points if you rent a bike for longer than the constraint you found in the previous question?</para>
            <para id="para-00057">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00058">Cycle Rentals</para>
            <para id="para-00059">Bikes Unlimited</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00006">
            <para id="para-00060">Correct! Cycle Rentals</para>
            <para id="para-00061">Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.4. </para>
            <para id="para-00062">The correct answer is Cycle Rentals.
      </para>
            <para id="para-00063">
        The constraint from question 5 is \(x=4\). 
        Since a number larger than 4 is needed to determine which shop offers more membership points if you rent a bike for longer than the constraint, choose to substitute in the number 5 for \(x\) to calculate how many membership points can be earned in 5 days.
        Since a number larger than 4 is needed to determine which shop offers more membership points if you rent a bike for longer than the constraint, choose to substitute in the number 5 for \(x\) to calculate how many membership points can be earned in 5 days.
        Cycle Rentals: \(20x=20(5)=100\) membership points
        Cycle Rentals: \(20x=20(5)=100\) membership points
        Bikes Unlimited: \(5x+60=5(5)+60=25+60=85\) membership points 
        Bikes Unlimited: \(5x+60=5(5)+60=25+60=85\) membership points 
      
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00005">
        <exercise id="_90b0f163-6e68-4323-abe1-441b5d71512c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00007">
            <para id="para-00064">7. Which number line represents the solution to the inequality, \(20x > 5x + 60\), representing the situation when you earn more membership points at Cycle Rentals?</para>
            <para id="para-00065">
              <figure id="fig-00007">
                <media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_07.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para id="para-00066">
              <figure id="fig-00008">
                <media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with a closed circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_08.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para id="para-00067">
              <figure id="fig-00009">
                <media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with a closed circle on the point 4. The number line to the left of 4 is shaded.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_09.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para id="para-00068">
              <figure id="fig-00010">
                <media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the left of 4 is shaded.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_10.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para id="para-00069">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00070">A.</para>
            <para id="para-00071">B.</para>
            <para id="para-00072">C.</para>
            <para id="para-00073">D.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00007">
            <para id="para-00074">Correct! <figure id="fig-00011"><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_11.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para id="para-00075">Try again. Revisit activities 2.9.2 and 2.9.3 </para>
            <para id="para-00076">The correct answer is<figure id="fig-00012"><media alt="A number line is shown from 2 to 6 with an open circle on the point 4. The number line to the right of 4 is shaded."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_12.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para id="para-00077">
        Solve for \(x\).
        \(20x \gt 5x+60\) 
        \(20x \gt 5x+60\) 
        \(15x \gt 60 \)
        \(15x \gt 60 \)
        \(x \gt 4\) 
        \(x \gt 4\) 
        The graph will have an open circle at 4, since the solution can not be equal to 4, with the arrow pointing to infinity to the right because the solution is greater than 4.
      
        The graph will have an open circle at 4, since the solution can not be equal to 4, with the arrow pointing to infinity to the right because the solution is greater than 4.
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="cbd8f0f5-868b-4eb0-907d-8d32d19e0c12" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00078">8. Which number line represents the solution to \(5+8x&lt;3(2x+4)\)?</para>
            <para id="para-00079">
              <figure id="fig-00013">
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_13.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para id="para-00080">
              <figure id="fig-00014">
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_14.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para id="para-00081">
              <figure id="fig-00015">
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_15.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para id="para-00082">
              <figure id="fig-00016">
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_16.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para id="para-00083">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00084">A.</para>
            <para id="para-00085">B.</para>
            <para id="para-00086">C.</para>
            <para id="para-00087">D.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00088">Correct! <figure id="fig-00017"><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_17.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para id="para-00089">Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.5 </para>
            <para id="para-00090">The correct answer is<figure id="fig-00018"><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_15_18.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
            <para id="para-00091">
        Solve for \(x\).
        \(5+8x \lt 3(2x+4)\)
        \(5+8x \lt 3(2x+4)\)
        \(5+8x \lt 6x+12\)
        \(5+8x \lt 6x+12\)
        \(5+2x \lt 12\)
        \(5+2x \lt 12\)
        \(2x \lt 7\) 
        \(2x \lt 7\) 
        \(x \lt \frac72\) 
        \(x \lt \frac72\) 
        \(x \lt 3\frac12\)
        \(x \lt 3\frac12\)
        The graph will have an open circle at \(x \lt 3\frac12\), since the solution can not be equal to \(3 \frac12\), with the arrow pointing to infinity to the left because the solution is less than \(3\frac12\).
      
        The graph will have an open circle at \(x \lt 3\frac12\), since the solution can not be equal to \(3 \frac12\), with the arrow pointing to infinity to the left because the solution is less than \(3\frac12\).
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_865a1d9b-7090-4ea4-97a2-8957c4880e3f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00009">
            <para id="para-00092">9. Find the solution set to this inequality by solving the related equation: \(2x-3>\frac{2x-5}2\)</para>
            <para id="para-00093">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00094">\(x&lt;\frac12\)</para>
            <para id="para-00095">\(x>\frac12\)</para>
            <para id="para-00096">\(x\leq\frac12\)</para>
            <para id="para-00097">\(x\geq\frac12\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00009">
            <para id="para-00098">Correct! \(x>\frac12\)</para>
            <para id="para-00099">Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.5 </para>
            <para id="para-00100">The correct answer is \(x>\frac12\). </para>
            <para id="para-00101">
        Solve for \(x\).
        \(2x−3\gt\frac{2x-5}{2}\)
        \(2x−3\gt\frac{2x-5}{2}\)
        \(4x−6\gt 2x-5\)
        \(4x−6\gt 2x-5\)
        \(2x−6\gt -5\)
        \(2x−6\gt -5\)
        \(2x\gt 1\)
        \(2x\gt 1\)
        \(x\gt\frac12 \)
      
        \(x\gt\frac12 \)
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_74ac8378-e680-406b-8f15-d884098ae579" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00010">
            <para id="para-00102">10. Here is an inequality: \(\frac{-10+x}4+5\geq\frac{7x-5}3\). What value of \(x\) will produce equality (or make the two sides equal)?</para>
            <para id="para-00103">\(x\) = ____ </para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00010">
            <para id="para-00104">Correct! 2</para>
            <para id="para-00105">Try again. Revisit activity 2.9.4.
      </para>
            <para id="para-00106">The correct answer is 2.
      </para>
            <para id="para-00107">
        Solve for \(x\).
        \(\frac{-10+x}{4}+5=\frac{7x-5}{3}\)
        \(\frac{-10+x}{4}+5=\frac{7x-5}{3}\)
        \(12(\frac{-10+x}{4}+5=\frac{7x-5}{3})\) 
        \(12(\frac{-10+x}{4}+5=\frac{7x-5}{3})\) 
        \(3(-10+x)+(12)(5)=4(7x-5)\)
      
        \(3(-10+x)+(12)(5)=4(7x-5)\)
      </para>
            <para id="para-00108">
        \(\begin{align*}
        -30+3x+60&amp;=28x-20 \\
        3x+30&amp;=28x-20\\
        3x+50&amp;=28x\\
        50&amp;=25x\\
        2&amp;=x\\
        \end{align*}\)
        The solution 2 will produce equality. It will make both sides equal.
      
        The solution 2 will produce equality. It will make both sides equal.
      </para>
            <para id="para-00109">
        \(\frac{-10+2}{4}+5\)
        \(\frac{-8}{4}+5\)
        \(\frac{-8}{4}+5\)
        \((-2+5)\)
        \((-2+5)\)
        3
      
        3
      </para>
            <para id="para-00110">
        \(\frac{(7)(2)-5}{3}\)
        \(\frac{14-5}{3}\)
        \(\frac{14-5}{3}\)
        \(\frac{9}{3}\)
        \(\frac{9}{3}\)
        3
      
        3
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
